---
// REVIEW: Layer 2 interpretation for the Chalder Fatigue Scale (CFQ-11),
// drafted for Craig's review. FAQ answers come from
// chalder-fatigue-scale.content.ts and are shown word for word (they also
// feed the page's FAQPage JSON-LD).
import { FAQ, REFERENCE, SOURCES } from '../../tools/chalder-fatigue-scale.content';
import '../../styles/interpretation.css';
const s = SOURCES;
---

<section class="interpretation" aria-labelledby="understanding-chalder">
	<h2 id="understanding-chalder">Understanding your Chalder Fatigue Scale score</h2>

	<h3 id="what-it-measures">What the score measures</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.scoring.a}</p>
	<p>
		It was developed in UK general practice, where its questions split into physical and mental fatigue
		(<a href={s.chalder1993.href} rel="noopener">{s.chalder1993.label}</a>); the same split appeared in a later study
		(<a href={s.cella2010.href} rel="noopener">{s.cella2010.label}</a>).
	</p>

	<h3 id="score-bands">Chalder Fatigue Scale score interpretation</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.normal.a}</p>
	<table class="interp-table">
		<caption>Likert reference points (0–33)</caption>
		<thead>
			<tr><th scope="col">Score</th><th scope="col">What it is</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			{REFERENCE.map(r => <tr><th scope="row">{r.score}</th><td>{r.group}</td><td><a href={s[r.source].href} rel="noopener">{s[r.source].label}</a></td></tr>)}
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		There are no published Likert bands. After COVID-19, 46% of the Norwegian group scored 4 or more on bimodal scoring
		(<a href={s.stavem2021.href} rel="noopener">{s.stavem2021.label}</a>).
	</p>
	<p>{FAQ.bimodal.a}</p>

	<h3 id="change">What a change in score means</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.change.a}</p>
	<p>
		The comparison point, "when you were last well", does not move, so after years of illness you are still comparing
		with your healthy self.
	</p>

	<h3 id="what-it-does-not-measure">What it does not measure</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.diagnosis.a}</p>
	<p>
		The <a href="/tools/fss/">Fatigue Severity Scale</a> measures how much fatigue gets in the way. For worsening after
		effort, use the <a href="/tools/dsq-pem/">DSQ-PEM</a> and see the
		<a href="/evidence/post-exertional-malaise/">post-exertional malaise explainer</a>.
	</p>

	<h3 id="taking-it-to-your-gp">Taking this to your GP or consultant</h3>
	<p>
		Bring the Likert total, the physical and mental subscores, the bimodal score, the date and the trend from your saved
		results. Papers and clinic letters quote either scoring, so give both. Describe it as a widely used 11-question
		fatigue questionnaire. It can show how much fatigue you report and how that changes; it cannot diagnose ME/CFS or
		long COVID. See
		<a href="/testing/preparing-for-gp-appointment/">preparing for a GP appointment</a>.
	</p>

	<div class="tool-foot">
		<slot name="disclaimer" />
		<details class="sources" id="sources">
			<summary>Sources and licence</summary>
			<slot name="citation" />
			<ul class="interp-sources">
				<li>Chalder T, Berelowitz G, Pawlikowska T, et al. Development of a fatigue scale. <em>J Psychosom Res</em> 1993;37(2):147–153. <a href={s.chalder1993.href} rel="noopener">Article</a></li>
				<li>Cella M, Chalder T. Measuring fatigue in clinical and community settings. <em>J Psychosom Res</em> 2010;69(1):17–22. <a href={s.cella2010.href} rel="noopener">Article</a> (averages as reported by <a href={s.hofmann2023.href} rel="noopener">{s.hofmann2023.label}</a> and other later papers)</li>
				<li>Stavem K, Ghanima W, Olsen MK, Gilboe HM, Einvik G. Prevalence and determinants of fatigue after COVID-19 in non-hospitalized subjects: a population-based study. <em>Int J Environ Res Public Health</em> 2021;18(4):2030. <a href={s.stavem2021.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
			</ul>
		</details>
	</div>
</section>
